# tools/check-rule-routes-mutations.json — 6 mutation(s), case command: bash tools/check-rule-routes-selftest.sh --case {case}
glob-matches-the-tree	routes#aGlobMatchingNoTrackedPathIsReported	every glob is matched against the tree's tracked paths, so a glob that matches nothing is reported instead of silently orphaning the rules a reader is told to read
rule-is-reached	routes#aRuleNoGlobReachesIsReported	every named rule under guidelines/ is compared with the rules the map reaches, so a rule no glob reaches is reported before it has a violation
change-names-the-derived-set	routes#theReadingSetFollowsTheDiff	a change may add a section to its derived reading set and never shrink it, so a derived section the change's artifacts do not name is reported
document-is-named	routes#aDocumentNoGlobNamesIsReported	every normative document under guidelines/ is matched against the map's globs, so a document no glob names is reported instead of deriving nothing for its own edits while its rules stay reached through another path's route
guideline-route-names-its-document	routes#aGuidelineRouteCarryingAnotherDocumentIsReported	a route whose glob sits under guidelines/ is compared with the documents its own entries name, so a route carrying a document its glob never reaches is reported rather than silently orphaning that document's reading set
route-names-its-document	routes#aGuidelineEditDerivesItsOwnDocument	a guideline glob names the document and its companion layers it owns, so one edit to one document derives that document's entries and not the corpus — with the UI family's glob reverted to the corpus-wide pattern, a Logging.md edit derives the UI, Design and Build families too, and a DesignStack.md edit derives the UI route
